{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>From the east.</b> To the east of Eden He planted the garden. And if you say: but it already wrote ״וַיִּבְרָא אֶת הָאָדָם וגוֹ׳״ — I found in the baraita of R. Eliezer son of R. Yosi HaGelili, among the 32 middot by which the Torah is expounded, and this is one of them: a general statement followed by its particulars — the particular is the specification of the first. ״וַיִּבְרָא אֶת הָאָדָם״ — this is a general statement; it left his creation unspecified from what, and left his deeds unspecified. Then it went back and specified: ״וַיִּיצֶר ה׳ אֱלֹהִים וגוֹ׳,״ and ״וַיַּצְמַח ה׳ אֱלֹהִים גַּן עֵדֶן״ and ״וַיָּנַח שָׁם אֶת הָאָדָם״ and ״וַיַּפֵּל ה׳ אֱלֹהִים תַּרְדֵּמָה״ — the listener might think this is another account, but it is only the specification of the first. And likewise with the beasts, it went back and wrote ״וַיִּצֶר ה׳ אֱלֹהִים מִן הָאֲדָמָה כָּל חַיַּת הַשָּׂדֶה,״ in order to specify ״וַיָּבֵא אֶל הָאָדָם״ — to call [them by] name — and to teach about the birds, that they were created from the mud."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:57:25.874391773Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}